  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\n- Dry through Tuesday\n\nOverall the theme of the forecast has not changed much in the past\ncouple of days. The first point remains the same in that we will\nsee dry weather through the weekend and at least into Tuesday. The\neast coast low will keep ridging in place across the Great Lakes\nboth at the surface and aloft. BUFKIT overviews remain very dry \nand all ensemble members of the GFS and ECWMF rain dry as well \nthrough Tuesday. Bottom line a high confidence forecast that\nconditions remain benign and dry for the next 4 days.\n\n- Rain chances increase mid week\n\nThe setup changes as we head into mid week. The reason for this is\nlargely a shortwave moving across Canada in zonal flow. The\nshortwave acts to flatten the ridge over the Great Lakes and pulls\nthe east coast system northward into the westerlies. What this\nmeans for the Great Lakes is some active weather as the ridge that\nwas over the region will be gone. \n\nModels show good chances for rain beginning as early as Tuesday\nnight, but more likely on Wednesday. At this point details are not\ncertain in the mid to late week time frame but it does look like\ngood opportunities for rain. The first opportunity will come on\nWednesday with a cold front associated with the aforementioned\nCanadian shortwave. The front looks to hang up across the Southern\nGreat Lakes with additional rain into Thursday and perhaps Friday.\n\nThe item of note here is that moisture in the mid and upper \nlevels, associated with what will be former Hurricane Polo, could\npotentially interact with the front in the Southern Great Lakes \nduring this time frame. PWATs are forecast by the GFS to reach \n2.00 inches which is very high/record for late Sept/early Oct. It\nis a long way out on day 5 and 6 so we have time to watch it, but\nit has our attention. Heavy rain events of the past have had \nsimilar setups with remnant moisture streaming northward from \nformer tropical systems. One event that comes to mind is September\nof 2008 when moisture from Gustav, Lowell and Ike all made it's\nway north into Lower Michigan. We are not by any means saying we \nare looking at an event like that, but some heavy rain is \npossible this upcoming week. 1-2 inches of rain seems plausible. \nIndividual ensemble members are indicating higher than that. \n\n- Above normal temperatures expected\n\nNormal high temperatures this time of year are in the 60s and for\nmuch of the 7 day forecast we are looking at highs in the 70s.",
